    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now honours an optional `Idempotency-Key` header: a repeat of a previously-seen key returns the originally created owner with **200** instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise 409).\n\n**New pieces**, each a small single-responsibility unit placed with its peers:\n\n- **`IdempotencyStore`** (`@Component`, owner package) \u2014 the one application-wide record of `key \u2192 owner id`. It's a shared singleton because keys are client-supplied and outlive any single request, so the mapping can't live in a per-request pipeline variable. `record` uses `putIfAbsent` so the first-created owner is the one replayed.\n- **`RecallIdempotentOwner`** \u2014 new **first** step. Reads the optional header via `ServerHttpConnection` (returns `null` when absent, so key-less requests are unaffected), and if the key was already used, escalates `IdempotentReplayException` *before* validation and the duplicate-identity check. Otherwise it publishes the key as a pipeline variable.\n- **`RecordIdempotentOwner`** \u2014 runs after `SaveOwner` (once the owner has its id), associating the key with that id. A key-less request records nothing.\n- **`IdempotentReplayException`** + **`IdempotentReplayExceptionHandler`** (+ escalation YAML) \u2014 the handler loads the original owner and returns it with 200, using the same representation a fresh create produces.\n\n**Reuse cleanup:** the DTO-building logic (`toOwnerDto` + bulk-signup warning) was duplicated across the two existing responders; I extracted it into a shared `OwnerResponses.toDto(...)` helper that all three response paths (201 create, 200 read, 200 replay) now call, rather than adding a third copy.\n\n**Notes on two decisions worth flagging:**\n- `@RequestHeader` does *not* bind in this OfficeFloor setup (I confirmed via logging that the key came through `null`), so I read the header through OfficeFloor's native `ServerHttpConnection` instead.\n- The replay is signalled through the escalation channel \u2014 the codebase's idiomatic early-terminate-and-respond mechanism \u2014 rather than a `@Flow` branch, because an OfficeFloor step's `next` still fires after a flow is invoked, which would double-send the response.",
